 and when you  do switch over to a c corp a delaware c  uh it's gonna just  be like paying your legal bills over  again and you have accounting issues  llcs are great for venture funds or  partnerships like a law partnership or  an accounting partnership with a bunch  of cpas or maybe you're buying real  estate assets because it has one time  taxation  and that's great but you don't have  shares in an llc  you have units and it's a completely  different structure they're for two  different types of entities so you can  talk to your lawyer about that you can  do some google searches but just for the  purposes of this checklist we don't  really need to debate it you need to be  a delaware c and you again when you're  raising from venture capitalists you  don't want to throw up red flags  having the wrong incorporation is like  walking into the room holding like five  red flags in each hand that you don't  know what you're doing  okay  item 72  do you have a cap table and is it clean  a messy cap table is a deal killer it is  a huge red flag for investors and it  often results in people not investing in  a company when they say the cap table is  broken or a messy cap table it means  that there are some people early on in  the cap table that have a very large  percentage of the company and maybe they  got it at a very low price and then the  founders ownership is way below 50  before they get to series a  when you're at a series a you really  want the founders to be above 50 percent  you have two founders 25 percent each of  three founders 15 to 18 each maybe even  20 percent each why does it need to be  there well we anticipate there'll be a  series b and people will be diluted 15  20 percent there'll be a series c maybe  you've diluted 10 to 15 percent and then  you do another round of funding and you  diluted another 10 you get the idea  you get diluted a couple of times you  might be down if you're a solo founder  at 20 by the time the company gets sold  or if you're two founders you might wind  up with seven eight nine percent of the  company when it goes public  that seems fine it seems fair you have  all this money put in the employees got  10 15 percent a bunch of investors got  the the bulk of the company they put a  ton of money to work  and uh maybe you sold some secondary  shares along the way but it needs to be  clean at the start and so pre-series a  founder is going to need to own 50 to 65  that's series a maybe they get cut to 40  50 like i was saying series b 30 40  series c 20 to 30 ipo exit 10 to 20  ownership  you don't want to give a large  percentage of your company to a dev shop  or consulting firm there are predatory  accelerators and dev shops and  consultants who will say hey  uh we'll give you 10k plus we'll build  your app which will cost five hundred  thousand dollars it'll take us six  months and uh we value your company at  two million so we now own  for the 100k  we own five percent and for the 500k we  own another 25 now they own 30 of your  company and now you're going into an  accelerator they get seven percent now  37 is owned by investors and then a seed  round happens you dilute 20  oh my god you know you're way behind the  numbers i just stated  so  uh we see this all the time it can be  fixed it's just painful so one of the  things that happens all the time is a  company that's done too much fundraising  will get to a new fundraising round and  the investor will say listen i will give  you 10 million dollars for your series a  for 25 of the company but the founders  are only at 30 i want to see them at 50  so therefore i want to do a founder  refresh and that's not coming out of my  25 that's coming out of everybody else's  and so boom now all your previous  investors get diluted they all feel  terrible about it  how do you mitigate against them feeling  terrible about it well  if they can participate in the new round  well then they can at least buy more  shares and keep their percentage  o